{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-13T17:10:57.970Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"1f65549d969a385dd658b1e6","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":null,"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.323","source":"probe"}},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments/1","value":{"created_at":"2026-11-13T17:10:57.969Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Review round 1 (Codex gpt-6.1-sol xhigh, Greptile): with the session-state lease reclaimable, a writer stalled past the TTL could overwrite newer state, because the write was not fenced. mutateSessionState now uses acquireLockLease and writes inside lease.fence, so a writer whose lease was replaced refuses with lock_lost (claim and release turn that into their attribution warning). The stale-lock next step ran pm gc against the invocation's own tracker when --pm-path selected another one: the cleanup step now derives the tracker from the lock path, adds --pm-path relative to the working directory when it differs from the resolved tracker, and names a manual removal when the lock root is not a tracker (the merge-driver lock under the git directory). New spec tests/unit/core/session/session-state-fence.spec.ts."}},{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-13T17:10:57.970Z"}],"before_hash":"a86a4c2c68dcd003fc973d472b8ab5b193a6bbcd6509573c70c091db870e933d","after_hash":"59c8a89d8751b64c2cf87d02fb7583ec3f161f7b2f72c6f35d813ac86ced79ac","item_hash_version":3,"context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"714169451f380487447dbfc8b0f